[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Four layers, built bottom-up, and semantics never live in a tool

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-LAYER-STACK
TITLE: Four layers, built bottom-up, and semantics never live in a tool
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: A layer is found to need something from a layer above it, or the four collapse to fewer in practice.
STATEMENT: >>>
The system is built in horizontal layers, one at a time, and each layer may use
only the layers beneath it. Vertical slices cut across them; a slice is how work
is delivered, a layer is how the system is reasoned about. The two axes are not
interchangeable and neither replaces the other.

  L0  GRAMMAR MACHINERY.  The typed Nix option surface, the consumer DSL, the
      check step and the emitter that render a .sgra file. Generic, knows
      nothing about this repository's node types. Delivered by milestone one.

  L1  THE GRAMMAR SPEC.  This repository's own vocabulary expressed through L0:
      which node types exist, what fields they carry, which relation roles they
      may make. Opinionated about node types, silent about behaviour.
      DEC-NODE-TYPE-GRID governs what belongs here.

  L2  HOOK MACHINERY.  Every place logic can attach, and how it is wired --
      what fires when a node is created, read, updated or deleted, and what a
      handler is handed. SEMANTIC-FREE AND UNOPINIONATED: it says where a rule
      could run, never what any rule is.

  L3  THE SEMANTIC LAYER.  Lifecycles, gates, ripple, signing, readiness. It is
      expressed as a DSL that DECOMPOSES into an L1 grammar plus L2 hooks, and
      it introduces no machinery of its own.

THE HARD RULE, and it has been violated once already: SEMANTICS ARE NEVER
WRITTEN DIRECTLY INTO A TOOL. A rule that lives in the body of a script rather
than in an L3 definition that decomposes onto L2 has skipped the layering, and
it is a violation regardless of whether the rule itself is correct. Correct
logic in the wrong layer is still the wrong layer; the judgement about the logic
is separate and comes later.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The operator's stated way of working: build one axis at a time, trust the layer
beneath, and do not reason about several axes at once. The value is not tidiness
-- it is that a layer with a clean boundary can be replaced, and a layer whose
semantics leaked into the layer below cannot.

Naming the layers explicitly also gives the violation a name. Without the
numbering, "you wired semantics into the tool" is a matter of taste and gets
relitigated every session; with it, the claim is checkable against a boundary
that was written down first.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Numbering settled 2026-08-28. Earlier sessions in this activity used "layer 0"
for the grammar SPEC and "layer 1" for node state machines. That numbering
predates recognising the Nix machinery as a layer of its own and is superseded
by the ladder above -- the old "layer 0" is L1 here, and the old "layer 1" is
part of L3, because a state machine is an opinion and opinions are semantic.

The milestone numbering in DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP is a DELIVERY order and
is not this. Milestones one and two delivered L0 and a writer over it; the next
session is L2; L3 is where the roadmap's milestones three through five live.
<<<
